WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.671
Defenders should think in terms of data flows, not only servers.

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:10.490
Sensitive data may move over internet traffic, VPN tunnels, API calls, email, file transfers,

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.399
cloud storage synchronization, database replication, partner integrations,

00:00:13.399 --> 00:00:17.970
service-to-service communication, mobile application traffic, and IoT or embedded device links.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.788
A server may be well managed, but the data path can still include networks,

00:00:23.788 --> 00:00:28.359
proxies, logs, archives, third-party services, and client environments that shape exposure.

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.762
TLS-protected web traffic is a common place to start because websites, APIs, gateways,

00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:36.671
and service meshes often carry valuable information.

00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:41.242
VPN traffic is also relevant because it may include administrative access,

00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:43.736
internal applications, or remote workforce activity.

00:00:43.736 --> 00:00:49.139
Email and file transfer systems can move legal records, contracts, health data, credentials,

00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:50.385
and business plans.

00:00:50.385 --> 00:00:56.203
Backup and replication traffic can carry large volumes of sensitive information over predictable paths.

00:00:56.203 --> 00:01:00.359
Logs and archives can matter as much as live traffic.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:04.931
A network appliance, proxy, packet capture platform, troubleshooting workflow, security tool,

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:09.502
or cloud service may preserve metadata or payloads longer than expected.

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.827
Third-party systems can also change the exposure model.

00:01:12.827 --> 00:01:16.982
If sensitive information crosses a partner integration, managed service, CDN,

00:01:16.982 --> 00:01:19.476
cloud storage platform, or SaaS product,

00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:24.047
the organization's risk depends partly on external cryptographic and retention practices.

00:01:24.047 --> 00:01:29.034
A practical assessment maps where sensitive data is created, transmitted, transformed, stored,

00:01:29.034 --> 00:01:30.281
replicated, and retained.

00:01:30.281 --> 00:01:34.437
That map should include encryption boundaries, termination points, certificate use,

00:01:34.437 --> 00:01:39.008
VPN endpoints, API gateways, service identities, backup flows, and vendor-managed paths.

00:01:39.008 --> 00:01:43.580
The goal is not to assume every path is equally urgent.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.567
The goal is to find the paths where long-lived sensitive data could

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:51.060
be collected today and exposed later.
